  "query_preview": "..\nMonday night through Tuesday: : Weak troughing over the Inland \nNorthwest will depart eastward today, transitioning to zonal flow \naloft over the Pacific Northwest tonight. A frontal system moving \ninto northern British Columbia will direct a moisture plume into the \nWashington Cascades, supported by a 120-125 kt upper-level jet \nstreak positioned north of the international border. This setup \nfavors orographically driven precipitation developing along the \nCascades early Tuesday, spreading into northeast Washington and \nnorth Idaho mountains Tuesday afternoon. Precipitation amounts will \nbe maximized across the west slopes of the Cascades and at the \nCascade crest with high confidence (greater than an 80% chance) for \namounts exceeding 0.50 inches. Strong westerly flow aloft into the \nCascades will produce a pronounced rain shadow east of the crest, \nwith amounts quickly dropping off farther east. Precipitation \namounts will be much lighter across northeast Washington with a 50% \nchance for 0.10 inches of rain across the northern Selkirks and a \n25% chance as far south as Mount Spokane. \n\nWinds will also be breezy on Tuesday as 850mb winds increase to 20-\n30 knots. The Inland Northwest will be within the warm sector with \nthe boundary layer only mixing up to around 800mb Tuesday afternoon. \nThis will limit the stronger winds at 700mb to materialize down to \nthe surface. Despite lower mixing heights, the National Blend of \nModels shows a 60-80% chance for wind gusts greater than 30 mph \nacross the Columbia Basin into the Spokane West Plains Tuesday \nafternoon. These gusts may result in patchy blowing dust near \nrecently plowed fields. \n\nWednesday through Sunday: A warming trend is anticipated as ensemble \nguidance depicts an upper-level ridge amplifying across the western \nUS. Uncertainty exists Thursday into Friday regarding the northward \nextent of the ridge axis. Approximately 45% of ensemble members \nfavor persistent zonal flow, which would direct another moisture \nplume into the Pacific Northwest with an additional period of light \nmountain precipitation. The remaining 55% of members build the ridge \nmore aggressively over the region, supporting a drier solution. \nEnsembles are in better consensus for the upper-level ridge to \nbecome established over the western US this weekend, resulting in \nwarm and dry conditions across the Inland Northwest. /vmt",
